WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.232
This query labels different phone states.

00:00:02.232 --> 00:00:04.542
A true SQL NULL is labeled NULL.

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:06.997
A trimmed empty string is labeled blank.

00:00:06.997 --> 00:00:11.687
All-zero or all-nine digit patterns are labeled sentinel for this teaching example.

00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:14.492
Everything else is treated as a candidate value.

00:00:14.492 --> 00:00:19.752
This classification step is useful before deciding whether any states should be normalized together.

00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:23.412
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:23.412 --> 00:00:27.747
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:30.837
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:30.837 --> 00:00:34.637
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:34.637 --> 00:00:40.682
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:40.682 --> 00:00:46.182
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:00:46.182 --> 00:00:48.240
not only whether the statement executes.
